  ],
  "details": "<p><em>The Barrington Atlas Directory</em> notes: RUS.\u00a0</p>\r\n<p>It should be noted that in Talbert's database of the <em>Tabula Peutingeriana</em>, two toponyms [<a class=\"ReferenceLink\" href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/us/talbert/talbertdatabase/TPPlace3057.html\" title=\" Talbert, Richard. \u201cDatabase: Peutinger Map Names and Features, with Commentary and User\u2019s Guide.\u201d In Explore the Peutinger Map. Cambridge UP, 2010. http://www.cambridge.org/us/talbert/talbertdatabase/prm.html. \">7A1 (Talbert 3057)</a>\u00a0<a class=\"ReferenceLink\" href=\"http://www.cambridge.org/us/talbert/talbertdatabase/TPPlace3090.html\" title=\" Talbert, Richard. \u201cDatabase: Peutinger Map Names and Features, with Commentary and User\u2019s Guide.\u201d In Explore the Peutinger Map. Cambridge UP, 2010. http://www.cambridge.org/us/talbert/talbertdatabase/prm.html. \">7A4 (Talbert 3090)</a>]\u00a0from the Peutinger map (Venedi and Venadi Sarmatae) point to a single place included in the\u00a0<em>Barrington Atlas </em>(BAtlas 2 H3 Venedi). The Trismegistos portal observes this same convention, noting the doublet. The University of Vienna Tabula Peutingeriana database, however, does not follow the same convention, creating one entry for each of the two Peutinger toponyms.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "The Venedi were an Indo-European people of central, continental Europe inhabiting lands to the east of the Vistula River.",
